nvhost: accept R32's fence names without CONFIG_SYNC

5.10 has no Android sync framework, so R35 makes nvhost's fences sync_file
fences, and its handler for NVHOST_IOCTL_CTRL_SYNC_FENCE_SET_NAME works
only with CONFIG_SYNC. JetPack 4's video converter names its fences and
stopped on the refusal.

Without CONFIG_SYNC the request now checks that the fd holds a fence made
only of host1x syncpoints, the check R32 made, and returns 0. The name is
not kept: a sync_file fence has none a driver can set, and R32 used it
only in debug output.

Found: trial 34. Tested on the Nano: in trial 35 the converter's set-name
request succeeded.

Licence: GPL-2.0-only. Lab notes, with every measurement: NOTES.md.
---
diff --git a/nvidia/drivers/video/tegra/host/host1x/host1x.c b/nvidia/drivers/video/tegra/host/host1x/host1x.c
index efacb138c..1ecc48b02 100644
--- a/nvidia/drivers/video/tegra/host/host1x/host1x.c
+++ b/nvidia/drivers/video/tegra/host/host1x/host1x.c
@@ -318,6 +318,23 @@ static int nvhost_ioctl_ctrl_sync_fence_set_name(
 
 	err = nvhost_sync_fence_set_name(args->fence_fd, name);
 	return err;
+#elif IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_TEGRA_GRHOST_SYNC)
+	/*
+	 * JetPack 4's (R32) video converter names the fences it creates; on
+	 * the Nano this request failed just before the converter stopped.
+	 * The sync_file fences used without CONFIG_SYNC keep no name of
+	 * their own, and the name only labelled the fence in sync debug
+	 * output, so check that the fd holds a syncpoint fence, as R32's
+	 * nvhost_sync_fdget() did, and leave the name out.
+	 */
+	struct nvhost_fence *fence = nvhost_fence_get(args->fence_fd);
+
+	if (!fence) {
+		nvhost_err(&ctx->dev->dev->dev, "failed to get fence");
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+	nvhost_fence_put(fence);
+	return 0;
 #else
 	nvhost_err(&ctx->dev->dev->dev, "operation not supported");
 	return -EINVAL;
